uh the uk is to become the first in the  world to allow  volunteers to be exposed to covid19  as part of a research trial to increase  our understanding of how the virus  affects people  as many as 90 volunteers aged between 18  and 30 are said to be taking part or  reporter becky cottrell  has more of the detail i mean this is  this is fascinating but one wonders  who and earth is going to be putting  themselves up for this trial  yeah well you say that but but actually  what they're looking at  with uh this study they're they're  actually going to expose the volunteers  to the original  form of the virus that really started to  circulate here in march 2020  and that original form the virus is seen  to be less dangerous  to adults between kind of 18 to 30 and  those are the people  that the researchers want to take part  in the study as you say it's a first of  its kind it's called a human  challenge study and it basically  involves deliberately exposing  volunteers to covid now that isn't a new  model it's been done before with malaria  and typhoid but it is the first time  it's been done with covert and the  reason it's taken us  so long to get to this point that the  trial is now coming to into effect  within the month  is because of those clear ethical  concerns around deliberately  exposing people to the virus they'll try  and make it as safe as possible the  volunteers will be studied 24 hours a  day  by medical professionals and what  they're really trying to look at is  how the immune system responds to the  virus but also what's going on with  vaccines and they'll be  they'll be using different vaccines that  have been approved to see  which is most effective at helping the  volunteers  to cope with covid and this really is a  vital research at a time when we're  trying to learn not only  how the virus works and what's going on  with different mutations  but also as the government is looking to  ease lockdown measures
 

